Recent hosp (about 10 days ago, local regional hospital) – discharge summary in letters:
- Dx: RLL pneumonia, type 2 resp failure, COPD exacerbation
- IV abx + steroids + nebs → home on 5/7 pred, 5/7 doxy, usual inhalers
- Suggested: GP review 1/52, repeat CXR 6–8/52, pulm rehab, smoking cessation, check inhaler technique

Today by phone:
Breathlessness “about the same as when I left hospital”. Gets SOB walking lounge → kitchen, rests, then ok. Mild cough, small amount white sputum, no blood. No chest pain, no fevers/rigors, no new ankle swelling. Sleeping “so‑so”, worse if lies flat without CPAP.

Non-adherence again:
- “Ran out of the blue puffer a few weeks ago… just used the grey one when I remembered.”
- Abx: “stopped a day early ‘cause I felt alright”.
- Pred: “might’ve missed a couple”.
- Has not booked pulm rehab or resp clinic – “didn’t get around to it”.
- CPAP “maybe three nights a week”, mask uncomfortable. Wife on phone briefly – says he “forgets half his puffers unless I nag him”.

Results (in MyHR/letters):
- CXR (8 days ago): RLL consolidation improving, no effusion, hyperinflated lungs c/w COPD. Radiology rec repeat CXR 6–8/52 to ensure resolution / exclude lesion.
- Bloods on discharge: WCC 9.8 (was 15 on admission per letter), CRP 18 and falling, U&E/LFTs normal.
- Spirometry ~3/12 ago: FEV1 45% predicted, FEV1/FVC 0.54 → mod–severe COPD (no prior spirometry values given to compare).

Telehealth “exam” (limited):
Speaking full sentences on phone, no audible wheeze, answers appropriately. Denies confusion, central chest pain, blue lips/fingers, marked leg swelling. Can manage ADLs slowly at home with rests. No SpO2 available.

Meds (per pt + discharge):
- Tiotropium 18mcg daily – “forget it half the time”
- Budesonide/formoterol 400/12, 2 puffs BD – often only takes “a couple of puffs when I’m tight”
- Salbutamol 100mcg pMDI PRN – currently none at home
- CPAP nocte – poor adherence as above
No known drug allergies.

Assessment (all interlinked really):
1) COPD mod–severe – post recent exacerbation, now clinically stable by history but still quite limited exercise tolerance and very poor adherence. High risk for further exacerbations.
2) Recent RLL pneumonia – improving clinically + on CXR, but not fully resolved; needs repeat imaging given age + smoking history (?underlying lesion).
3) OSA – suboptimal CPAP use, likely contributing to fatigue and nocturnal SOB.
4) Non-adherence to meds/follow-up – major issue; pt admits “I just forget” / “couldn’t be bothered going back in”.

